dex": -1, "case_paths": ["/wis/68/0530-01"]}, {"cite": "30 Ark., 326", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Ark.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "109 N. Y., 43", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "N.Y.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "33 Cal., 60", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Cal.", "case_ids": [2264226], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/cal/33/0060-01"]}, {"cite": "42 Tex., 44", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Tex.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "68 Mo., 203", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Mo.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "5 Ark., 432", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Ark.", "weight": 2, "pin_cites": [{"page": "518"}], "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "3 Bay., 94", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Bay", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "74 Cal., 570", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Cal.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "47 S. E., 140", "category": "reporters:state_regional", "reporter": "S.E.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "35 S. E., 214", "category": "reporters:state_regional", "reporter": "S.E.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "74 S. C., 415", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4428750], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/74/0415-01"]}, {"cite": "68 S. C., 258", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4420897], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/68/0258-01"]}, {"cite": "56 S. C., 540", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [9504445], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/56/0540-01"]}, {"cite": "51 S. C., 284", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [8715997], "pin_cites": [{"page": "28"}], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/51/0284-01"]}, {"cite": "13 S. C., 439", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4374357, 4374429], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/13/0439-01", "/sc/13/0439-02"]}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 289, "char_count": 3409, "ocr_confidence": 0.471, "pagerank": {"raw": 2.79524831683491e-07, "percentile": 0.8363261719119531}, "sha256": "76e290c28732fd6f672826372ea78c0f2222429647514edddbc1cdeecde05d1b", "simhash": "1:4529777aae96bd24", "word_count": 624}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T20:09:51.886260+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["STATE v. BYARS."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Chief Justice Pope.\nAt the September term of t\u00edre Court of General Sessions for Richland, the defendant, R. F. Byars, was put upon his trial for murder, and the jury found him guilty; whereupon the defendant made a motion for a new trial upon the following five ground's :\n1. \u201cBecause the evidence does not support the verdict.\n2. \u201cBecause his Honor erred, as' a matter of law, in not directing the defendant to accompany the jury in viewing the premises and not including in the order that the defendant, as well as his counsel, should accompany the jury.\n3. \u201cBecause -his Honor erred, as a matter of law, in not accompanying the jury to the scene of the alleged difficulty, as that was a part of the trial.\n4. \u201cBecause his Honor erred, as a matter of law, in asking in the presence of the jury, whether or not the defendant\u2019s counsel objected to the jury examining the premises.\n5. \u201cIt is respectfully submitted that the 'Cburt erred in allowing Sheriff 'Coleman and\u2019 E. C. Knox, 'his constable, before either had been sworn as witnesses in the case, to go .with the jury to the alleged place of the homicide, the defendant being absent and having no 'Opportunity to cross-examine the said Coleman and Knox as to their statements as to the locality.\u201d\nHis Honor, George Johnstone, Special Judge, in sustaining the motion- for a new trial upon these five ground's attaches great importance to soi much of them as relate to the absence of the Court and the defendant while the jury, in charge of the sheriff and his deputy were carried to view the locus in quo of the homicide.\nThe question' is not only important but very interesting, but we feel ourselves- unable at this time to entertain a consideration of the questions now presented. This Court is confined to the 'consideration- of questions presented -after a final judgment has been rendered.\nThis is no new question to this Court, for we have held that a final judgment is essential -in the hearing of an appeal. The prisoner has never been -sentenced; the sentence is a final judgment. Our cases of State v. McKettrick, 13 S. C., 439; State v. Burbage, 51 S. C., 284, 28. S. E., 937; State v. Hughes, 56 S. C., 540, 35 S. E., 214; State v. Timmons, 68 S. C., 258. 47 S. E., 140, and State v. Hill, 74 S. C., 415, so hold.\nThe judgment of this Court is, that the appeal be dismissed and -the case remanded' to the Circuit Court for a new trial.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Chief Justice Pope."}], "attorneys": ["Solicitor George Bell Timmerman, for appellant.", "Mr. R. B. Carwile, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "6764\nSTATE v. BYARS.\nAppeal. \u2014 In criminal case appeal after verdict lies only from sentence.\nBefore George Johnstone, SpEciar Judge, Richland,\nSeptember, 1907.\nAppeal dismissed.\nIndictment against R. F. Byars for murder of.......... . . . .i. ,|.Stale appeals' from motion granting- new trial.\nSolicitor George Bell Timmerman, for appellant.\nOral argument.\nMr. R. B. Carwile, contra,\ncites: Is viewing locus in quo taking evidence? 133 Oal., 171; 70 Mis\u00ae., 755; 74 Cal., 570; 3 Bay., 94; 24 Ra. Ann., 47; 5 Ark., 432; 10 Id., 518; 19 Id., 209; Whar. Cr. PI. & Pr., 707; 68 Mo., 203; 42 Tex., 44; 3 Bask., 25; 33 Cal., 60'; 109 N. Y., 43; 30 Ark., 326; 68 Wis., 530; 103' S. W. R., 780; 13 Atl., 774. Prisoner should be present: 36 S. C., 506; 22 W. Va., 800; 20 Ohio, 31; 32 Tex., 430. Waiver of right to be present: 110 U. S., 518. This order, is not appealable: 13 S. C., 439; 51 S. C., 284; 56 S. C., 540; 68 S. C., 258; 74 S. C., 415.\nFebruary 25, 1908."}, "file_name": "0174-01", "first_page_order": 186, "last_page_order": 187}